1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,102
Lesson 4.3.

2
00:00:02,482 --> 00:00:03,503
MCMC:

3
00:00:03,883 --> 00:00:05,825
Metropolis–Hastings and Gibbs.

4
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:11,188
We already know we cannot compute the
partition function of a large model.

5
00:00:11,568 --> 00:00:15,520
The way out is to sample: build
a Markov chain whose stationary

6
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:19,722
distribution is exactly the Boltzmann
distribution, and let it run.

7
00:00:20,102 --> 00:00:23,058
That is the Markov chain
Monte Carlo method.

8
00:00:23,438 --> 00:00:27,181
In this lesson we will see its
two classic recipes and how to

9
00:00:27,181 --> 00:00:30,679
parallelize them, which is
exactly what a pbit chip does.

10
00:00:31,783 --> 00:00:35,144
Metropolis proposes a
change, for example flipping

11
00:00:35,144 --> 00:00:37,966
a spin, and computes the energy change ΔE.

12
00:00:38,346 --> 00:00:40,577
If the energy goes
down, it always accepts.

13
00:00:40,957 --> 00:00:44,962
If it goes up, it accepts
with probability e^(−βΔE).

14
00:00:45,342 --> 00:00:49,815
With that rule, the probability flow
between two states is equal in both

15
00:00:49,815 --> 00:00:53,977
directions: detailed balance holds
with the Boltzmann distribution.

16
00:00:54,357 --> 00:00:56,782
The nice part is that we only need energy

17
00:00:56,782 --> 00:00:59,266
differences, never the partition function.

18
00:01:00,383 --> 00:01:03,908
Gibbs sampling takes one
variable and redraws it from

19
00:01:03,908 --> 00:01:07,300
its conditional distribution,
given all the others.

20
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:11,688
By the Markov blanket, that conditional
depends only on the neighbors.

21
00:01:12,068 --> 00:01:20,203
For an Ising spin it is P(s_i = +1) =
σ(2βu_i), a sigmoid of the local field.

22
00:01:20,583 --> 00:01:24,880
There are no rejections: each step
produces a new sample of that variable.

23
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:29,306
And the sigmoid of the local field
is exactly what a pbit implements.

24
00:01:30,417 --> 00:01:33,291
Updating spins one by one is slow.

25
00:01:33,671 --> 00:01:35,693
Can we update many at once?

26
00:01:36,073 --> 00:01:38,461
Yes, as long as they
are not neighbors: their

27
00:01:38,461 --> 00:01:40,638
conditionals do not depend on each other.

28
00:01:41,018 --> 00:01:44,532
We color the graph so that
no two neighbors share a

29
00:01:44,532 --> 00:01:48,184
color, and update all nodes
of one color in parallel.

30
00:01:48,564 --> 00:01:52,740
On a square lattice two colors
suffice, like a chessboard.

31
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:56,488
That is chromatic Gibbs, and
that is how a pbit chip works.

32
00:01:57,617 --> 00:02:00,444
And if we update all spins simultaneously?

33
00:02:00,824 --> 00:02:05,162
Each would use the old value of its
neighbors, which are also changing.

34
00:02:05,542 --> 00:02:09,720
The resulting chain no longer has
Boltzmann as its stationary distribution.

35
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:14,482
At temperature three,
chromatic Gibbs gives a mean

36
00:02:14,482 --> 00:02:18,689
energy per spin of −0.82,
and Metropolis, −0.79.

37
00:02:19,069 --> 00:02:24,304
The fully parallel update
gives 0.02: completely wrong.

38
00:02:25,417 --> 00:02:27,508
An example of Metropolis acceptance.

39
00:02:27,888 --> 00:02:31,344
On a square lattice at
temperature three, flipping a spin

40
00:02:31,344 --> 00:02:35,225
surrounded by four aligned neighbors
raises the energy by eight.

41
00:02:35,605 --> 00:02:40,667
The acceptance probability is
e^(−8/3), about seven percent.

42
00:02:41,047 --> 00:02:44,868
The same spin, with
Gibbs, ends up against its

43
00:02:44,868 --> 00:02:49,354
neighbors with probability
σ(−8/3), about 6.5 percent.

44
00:02:49,734 --> 00:02:54,135
The two chains differ in their
steps, but agree on the distribution.

45
00:02:55,250 --> 00:03:00,737
Pbit chips implement chromatic Gibbs
in hardware, one color per clock phase.

46
00:03:01,117 --> 00:03:05,892
THRML does block Gibbs
over factor graphs on GPU.

47
00:03:06,272 --> 00:03:09,465
And since the graph has
bounded degree, few colors

48
00:03:09,465 --> 00:03:12,148
suffice, which allows massive parallelism.

49
00:03:12,528 --> 00:03:15,339
In lab five you will program it in JAX.

50
00:03:16,450 --> 00:03:19,540
Frequent mistakes: updating
neighbors at the same time.

51
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:23,432
Using a non-symmetric proposal
without the Hastings correction.

52
00:03:23,812 --> 00:03:26,139
Forgetting the factor
of two in the sigmoid.

53
00:03:26,519 --> 00:03:29,773
And confusing a correct
stationary distribution with

54
00:03:29,773 --> 00:03:32,964
fast mixing, which is the
topic of the next lesson.

55
00:03:34,083 --> 00:03:34,916
In summary:

56
00:03:35,296 --> 00:03:40,040
Metropolis accepts changes with
probability min(1, e^(−βΔE)),

57
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:44,161
and Gibbs redraws each
variable from its conditional.

58
00:03:44,541 --> 00:03:49,194
Both satisfy detailed balance with
Boltzmann and use only local information.

59
00:03:49,574 --> 00:03:53,814
Coloring the graph allows parallel
updates without breaking the distribution.

60
00:03:54,917 --> 00:03:58,591
To practice: prove detailed
balance for Metropolis.

61
00:03:58,971 --> 00:04:01,533
Derive the Gibbs conditional for Ising.

62
00:04:01,913 --> 00:04:03,983
Count the colors of a triangular lattice.

63
00:04:04,363 --> 00:04:07,812
And explain why the fully
parallel update fails.
